SAURABH GUPTA: So
welcome to NET303--

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:12.190
Livin' on the Edge.

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:14.230
I actually wanted to
have that song playing,

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:17.590
but twas not to be.

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:20.950
Just a quick reminder,
please do fill out the Dory

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:21.950
if you have questions.

00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:24.010
That's how we will be
in taking questions.

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:25.552
Of course, there
are microphones here

00:00:25.552 --> 00:00:28.420
if you want to have just
a live Q&amp;A. It will really

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:32.150
help us record the questions
and see what kinds of interests

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:36.490
our folks have in the field.

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:38.590
And with that let's
go in to introductions

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:40.150
so my name is Saurabh Gupta.

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:42.630
I am a PM on Cloud CDN.

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:46.142
And with me here is esteemed
friend and colleague, Brian.

00:00:46.142 --> 00:00:47.350
BRIAN ROGAN: I'm Brian Rogan.

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:50.500
I'm Senior director
at Google for CDN

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:54.340
and engineering for the
internet facing network as well

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:55.663
as interconnect.

00:00:55.663 --> 00:00:57.580
So I think I'm going to
take it away from here

00:00:57.580 --> 00:00:59.140
and walk you through our agenda.

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:02.320
We're going to talk a little bit
about the background of Google

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.680
CDN I'll take you through a
little bit about how it works

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.320
under the hood, and how we
think about the content delivery

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:09.813
problem at Google.

00:01:09.813 --> 00:01:11.980
Saurabh is going to take
you through how to actually

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:13.802
get everything up
and running yourself.

00:01:13.802 --> 00:01:15.760
And then we'll talk a
little bit about security

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:17.560
and how you can use
what we built to be

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:20.890
successful in your business.

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:24.700
So to start off, and I think
one of the things that TK talked

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:27.478
about a lot on Tuesday all
the way back at the beginning,

00:01:27.478 --> 00:01:28.270
was about openness.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:29.860
And Google Cloud
is about openness.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:32.710
And CDN at Google
is no different.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:35.560
Well, today we're going to talk
primarily about our first party

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:36.700
CDN offering.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:39.720
We work with a series of amazing
CDN interconnect partners.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:41.900
We can offer you preferential
pricing on cache flow,

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:43.317
so that at Google,
you know you're

00:01:43.317 --> 00:01:46.570
going to have the CDN that
satisfies your needs best.

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:49.242
It's not about lock in, it's not
about pushing you towards us.

00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:50.950
It's about the choice
that works for you.

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:57.740
Let's talk about where we see
Google CDN being different.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:00.212
First off, I think it comes
down a lot to performance.

00:02:00.212 --> 00:02:01.670
That's kind of our
headline, that's

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:02.960
what we've been working
on a lot of Google

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:04.570
over the last decade in CDN.

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:08.539
Is how do we have the
broadest reach, the deepest

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:10.303
connectivity, and
great performance.

00:02:10.303 --> 00:02:12.720
And I think the numbers sort
of speak for themselves here,

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:15.690
both in terms of
throughput and latency.

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:19.503
So we think we really have a
best in class product on CDN

00:02:19.503 --> 00:02:21.170
when you think about
performance and you

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:25.030
think about how to reach your
users all over the globe.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:27.358
We think the products also
really marked by simplicity.

00:02:27.358 --> 00:02:29.650
Saurabh I'll take you through
this in a lot more depth.

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:32.080
But you can turn on CDN at
Google with a single checkbox.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:34.630
And with that checkbox, you
get all of our deployment.

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:38.320
You get you can reach all around
the world, and you get CDN,

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.300
you get caching.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:42.860
And let's look at that reach.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:44.930
So you we have 134
points of presence.

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:47.240
And again, for the
last north of a decade,

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:49.660
we've been investing in
our physical infrastructure

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:52.220
and in a network that's going
to get you around the world

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:53.865
and get you close to your users.

00:02:53.865 --> 00:02:55.490
Again, we will dive
actually a fair bit

00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:56.907
deeper into this,
and how we think

00:02:56.907 --> 00:03:00.360
about this part of the problem.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.160
But along with that,
you also get scale.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.020
And you get the
infrastructure that Google

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:06.270
uses for its own
first party services.

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:09.480
And we have seven products
with over a billion users each

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:10.613
all around the world.

00:03:10.613 --> 00:03:12.030
And again, we'll
dive a little bit

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:16.390
deeper on how we think about
that part of the problem.

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:19.080
So to jump in a
little bit and talk

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.720
about our network architecture.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.880
I want to reflect
back on when we

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.740
started this journey at Google.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:29.370
The year was 2006, we had
just acquired YouTube.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:32.490
And at that time, if you could
make it through a YouTube video

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:35.280
without it rebuffering, it
was like a small miracle.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:36.810
We kind of celebrate.

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:38.807
I ruined many a Friday
night social gathering

00:03:38.807 --> 00:03:40.890
by trying to watch YouTube
with friends, you know,

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:43.200
popping out my TCP
dump, and grabbing

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:44.855
logs when things didn't work.

00:03:44.855 --> 00:03:46.230
It was a super
awkward experience

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:47.860
for everyone involved.

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:50.160
I was not invited back.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.270
But if you flash
forward a decade,

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:54.720
I think one of our
greatest accomplishments

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:57.150
is the fact that for the
vast majority of users

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.130
and for the vast majority
of workloads now,

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:01.530
connectivity blends into
the background, right?

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:04.200
I watch the Super Bowl on
YouTube TV five seconds

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.110
behind the [? MES ?] feed for
three hours without a rebuffer,

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:07.860
this year, right?

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:10.800
And as a user, I didn't even
think about Right I just

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.390
assumed that that would work.

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:14.880
And so I think what
we've seen over this time

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:17.130
is like an absolutely
transformative experience

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:20.130
in terms of what it means to be
on the receiving end of content

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:20.890
delivery.

00:04:20.890 --> 00:04:22.710
And I think what that means
for you, for our customers,

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:24.000
is that all of a
sudden, you can build

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.415
all of these amazing experiences
where connectivity just

00:04:26.415 --> 00:04:27.540
gets out of the way, right?

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.250
Where the users are not
distracted by the fact

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:30.667
that things are
buffering, they're

00:04:30.667 --> 00:04:31.920
not distracted by waiting.

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:34.350
They can really engage
with your content,

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:37.630
and they can really kind of
work closely with your business.

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:39.518
So I wanted to talk a
little bit about how

00:04:39.518 --> 00:04:41.310
we think about the
content delivery problem

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:42.930
and what got us here,
just to give you

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:44.850
some background about
what's underneath the hood

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:45.850
of that single checkbox.

00:04:48.513 --> 00:04:50.430
So when we think about
how to reach the globe,

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:52.230
you know, step one is
kind of the obvious.

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:54.630
You need to go
where the users are.

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:57.030
And so the main challenge of
the content delivery problem

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.315
is kind of content
lives in data centers,

00:04:59.315 --> 00:05:00.690
and users live
everywhere, right?

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:03.450
And so the goal is to figure
out how we can, with the best

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:06.540
performance possible, get your
content from our data centers

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:09.600
directly into the last mile,
and into your users' devices,

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:11.300
and into their homes.

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:14.910
And so first thing first,
we go where the internet is.

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:16.920
We work with anybody.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:19.680
We connect to the vast
majority of networks

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:24.550
of last mile user facing
networks on the globe directly.

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:26.670
And that's a point
of pride of ours.

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:28.740
And it's a core part
of how we got here

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:32.280
and how we built what we have.

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:34.560
We run it at massive
scale at Google scale,

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:37.080
and so again, cloud
CDN product is

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:40.110
a product that leverages all of
the scale of the infrastructure

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:42.000
that we've built at Google.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.850
And you kind of ride along
and you get that for free.

00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:48.040
And finally, I'll talk
a little bit about this.

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:49.800
We've spent the last
few years figuring

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:52.270
out how can we innovate in
the content delivery problem.

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:53.580
How can we innovate in network?

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:55.290
And how can we make
it so that you really

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:57.198
have a very unique set
of capabilities here

00:05:57.198 --> 00:05:58.740
that push forward
the whole internet.

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:04.070
Just to go back again, and
to talk about that kind

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:06.046
of content delivery problem.

00:06:06.046 --> 00:06:09.260
You know kind of where
our data centers are.

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:11.540
You also have a sense
of in all of our edge

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:14.542
points of presence or places
where we have CDN caching.

00:06:14.542 --> 00:06:17.000
And so I thought maybe it would
be kind of cool to take you

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:18.650
through a little bit
of that journey of how

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:20.260
your content starts
at a data center

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:23.130
and how it gets out
to the internet.

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:25.910
So I figured I would start with
this beautiful slide of one

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:27.680
of our data centers at sunrise.

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:30.570
And well, it is gorgeous,
and I would love to be there.

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:33.200
You will notice there are
not a great many users there.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:35.400
And so that's sort of one
of the central challenges

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:36.765
out of that in general.

00:06:36.765 --> 00:06:38.390
Data centers on the
internet are placed

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:41.210
in places where you can
acquire a couple hundred acres

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:42.860
and like, a gigawatt of power.

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:46.170
Not necessarily right in
the middle of city centers.

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:48.170
And I think this is an
important kind of insight

00:06:48.170 --> 00:06:50.628
because if you think about how
the internet is provisioned,

00:06:50.628 --> 00:06:52.520
how the internet works,
it's all around kind

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:53.850
of the centers of population.

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:55.010
It's all around the
places that you'd want

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:56.370
to go out on a Friday night--

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:59.860
the New Yorks, and Londons,
and Parises of the world.

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:01.970
And so there's quite
a journey to sometimes

00:07:01.970 --> 00:07:06.870
be had from where your content
lives to where your users are.

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:10.460
So it starts at the
data centers, right?

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:13.820
But at Google-- and I think
compared to some other people--

00:07:13.820 --> 00:07:16.130
content delivery is a
ground up problem for us.

00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:17.990
And we literally think
about it as how do we

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:20.360
get from where
your content lives,

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:22.250
from where you're
workloads live, all the way

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:23.635
through to the internet.

00:07:23.635 --> 00:07:25.010
And so here we
have some pictures

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:26.177
of some of the construction.

00:07:26.177 --> 00:07:28.550
This is all first party stuff
that we've done a Google.

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:30.970
And up on the top left,
which I think is really cool,

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:33.318
is us burying duct bank.

00:07:33.318 --> 00:07:35.610
And you'll see there there's
a whole bunch of conduits,

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:38.930
and they actually get strung
together in a metal frame

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:40.470
to make that cool square.

00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:42.110
And then we didn't show the part
where they fill the whole thing

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:42.818
in with concrete.

00:07:42.818 --> 00:07:46.670
Like, miles and miles
and miles of concrete.

00:07:46.670 --> 00:07:51.140
So just think that
in this world,

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:53.540
this is an intensely
physical process.

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:56.450
That's one of the things I like
to kind of remark about in CDN,

00:07:56.450 --> 00:07:58.490
is that for most
people, the internet is

00:07:58.490 --> 00:07:59.930
this very ethereal thing.

00:07:59.930 --> 00:08:02.300
It kind of lives
off in the cloud.

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:05.510
But in our world, I mean,
it is concrete, and conduit,

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:06.590
and pulling fiber.

00:08:06.590 --> 00:08:09.410
And it is a very physical thing.

00:08:09.410 --> 00:08:11.930
On the bottom right, you
have a little bit more

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:14.690
of an easier of long haul
pole with a little bit less

00:08:14.690 --> 00:08:17.860
fiber, where we actually just
do kind of open trenching

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:19.310
that we cover in.

00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:22.130
But again, these are the
kinds of physical investments

00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:25.642
that power this
network that you see.

00:08:25.642 --> 00:08:27.850
Obviously, we have a
significant subsea investments--

00:08:27.850 --> 00:08:31.690
13 CDN investments that we've
done either through consortia

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:33.520
or with other folks.

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:36.429
And if you've never seen how
a submarine cable is laid,

00:08:36.429 --> 00:08:38.140
it is one of the
most blowing things

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:39.340
that I have seen in my life.

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:40.990
I mean, we have a
gigantic cable ship

00:08:40.990 --> 00:08:43.480
that literally runs cable
across the entire ocean,

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:46.090
lays it down on the ocean
floor through kind of valleys

00:08:46.090 --> 00:08:49.060
and ridges, trying
to figure out how

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:51.220
we can make sure that
we have a clean path

00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:53.030
underneath the ocean.

00:08:53.030 --> 00:08:55.330
But the craziest part is
it's like not just passive.

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:57.550
All of this light
needs to be repeated

00:08:57.550 --> 00:08:58.962
every 100 kilometers or so.

00:08:58.962 --> 00:09:00.670
And so there's literally
active equipment

00:09:00.670 --> 00:09:01.933
that lives underwater.

00:09:01.933 --> 00:09:04.350
And when these things break
or when they need to be fixed,

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:06.220
we send UAVs down and
pull the cable up,

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:09.170
then cable tents for miles
because of trigonometry.

00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:11.650
So these kind of just
absolutely amazing things

00:09:11.650 --> 00:09:12.850
that happen under the hood.

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:17.265
All kind of sight unseen to
you the user of our service.

00:09:17.265 --> 00:09:18.640
Then when we talk
about, OK, what

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:20.890
does it mean to like to meet
the internet where it is.

00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:22.490
The internet is all
around the world.

00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:25.900
And so this is actually what
maybe one of our 134 POPs

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:27.235
looks like.

00:09:27.235 --> 00:09:29.110
And you'll notice compared
to the next slide,

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:30.818
everything here is
very clean and cabled.

00:09:30.818 --> 00:09:32.950
But in the bottom
right, this is actually

00:09:32.950 --> 00:09:35.260
what one of our
peer facing fabrics

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:37.658
looks like, just cabled up.

00:09:37.658 --> 00:09:39.700
And the thing that you
have to keep in mind again

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:40.810
is just the scale.

00:09:40.810 --> 00:09:43.030
These are sort of
two routers that

00:09:43.030 --> 00:09:47.200
are part of the fabric
in one of 134 POPs.

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:49.990
And so this is just a massive,
massive globally distributed

00:09:49.990 --> 00:09:53.410
infrastructure, with just
hundreds of thousands

00:09:53.410 --> 00:09:57.010
of pairs of fiber, all of
them meticulously labeled,

00:09:57.010 --> 00:09:57.770
you'll note.

00:09:57.770 --> 00:10:00.190
Which I don't know how anyone
has the patience to do that,

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:02.340
but I thank them for it.

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:04.840
And so that's kind of what the
physical infrastructure looks

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:06.370
like on the ground.

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:08.820
And all of that is to
actually meet the internet.

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:10.990
And you'll notice now we're
kind of outside of space

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:11.890
that's controlled by Google.

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:13.990
But when you think about
how the internet actually

00:10:13.990 --> 00:10:16.150
works in the real
world, it's literally

00:10:16.150 --> 00:10:18.370
about two different
networks connecting

00:10:18.370 --> 00:10:19.672
to one another physically.

00:10:19.672 --> 00:10:22.130
And so we're actually creating
an optical path between say,

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:25.450
Google, and a network that can
help us deliver that content.

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:26.950
And it's sometimes
a messy business.

00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:30.310
This is a Meet Me room at one
of our colocation facilities,

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:34.000
where basically
networks that connect

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:35.830
to user traffic and
Google will actually

00:10:35.830 --> 00:10:38.710
meet up, make a fiber
path, and kind of link back

00:10:38.710 --> 00:10:40.528
to our infrastructure.

00:10:40.528 --> 00:10:42.070
So this is sort of
the messy business

00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:45.990
of how the internet
works under the hood.

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:48.300
And I think just to talk
a little about-- this

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:50.400
is the result. Is
kind of what you

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:52.350
see with Google network tiers.

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:55.120
This is the premium network
that we keep talking about

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:56.160
and what it means.

00:10:56.160 --> 00:11:00.150
Where you can actually ride
our first party infrastructure

00:11:00.150 --> 00:11:03.760
right from your data center
all the way to the end user.

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:06.595
We also offer for more kind
of value minded customers

00:11:06.595 --> 00:11:08.220
or for workloads that
don't necessarily

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:11.167
need the performance of that
premium tier, a standard tier

00:11:11.167 --> 00:11:12.750
product which rides
over the internet,

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:14.208
and doesn't
necessarily get to take

00:11:14.208 --> 00:11:19.560
advantage of all of that
privately built infrastructure.

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:21.170
But you can see the difference.

00:11:21.170 --> 00:11:23.462
When we look at what does it
mean to be on that premium

00:11:23.462 --> 00:11:25.650
network, in this
particular test,

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:28.890
we can actually see an almost
2x improvement in throughput.

00:11:28.890 --> 00:11:33.750
And so those ground up
investments really matter.

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:36.330
And again, I think that's for
me the main takeaway here.

00:11:36.330 --> 00:11:38.130
Is that when you
come to Google, you

00:11:38.130 --> 00:11:41.040
have an infrastructure that's
built literally from the dirt

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:43.090
right up to give you
the best performance.

00:11:43.090 --> 00:11:45.910
And you can see it.

00:11:45.910 --> 00:11:47.900
I want to talk a
little bit about what

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:49.610
we were busy doing
on the software side

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:52.220
for that decade to get us there.

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:54.950
And here, we also I think, had
a lot of really interesting

00:11:54.950 --> 00:11:56.987
innovations over that time.

00:11:56.987 --> 00:11:58.820
One of the areas that
we've worked a lot in,

00:11:58.820 --> 00:12:02.660
because there's such high
leverage, is area of transport.

00:12:02.660 --> 00:12:04.520
So the protocols that
actually make sure we

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:07.405
can reliably deliver
traffic over the internet.

00:12:07.405 --> 00:12:09.530
So QUIC is something we've
invested a lot of energy

00:12:09.530 --> 00:12:12.230
in over time it originated
here, although it's

00:12:12.230 --> 00:12:15.055
an open standard
used by many people.

00:12:15.055 --> 00:12:17.700
And we worked a lot on how
do we get latency down.

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:19.370
And so we have the
zero RTT handshake

00:12:19.370 --> 00:12:20.787
that allows you
to kind of quickly

00:12:20.787 --> 00:12:22.650
establish a
connection to Google.

00:12:22.650 --> 00:12:24.230
And you can see
what it meant to us.

00:12:24.230 --> 00:12:25.910
When we flipped
on QUIC en masse,

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:30.150
we saw a significant improvement
in throughput across the board.

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:31.820
And so I think this
has been really one

00:12:31.820 --> 00:12:34.160
of the key drivers of sort
of innovation of the protocol

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:36.110
level for us.

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:38.270
We also worked a lot
on congestion control,

00:12:38.270 --> 00:12:40.850
and that was agnostic
to TCP and QUIC.

00:12:40.850 --> 00:12:43.430
Both implement congestion
avoidance protocol

00:12:43.430 --> 00:12:44.840
that we called BBR.

00:12:44.840 --> 00:12:47.660
And that works on the insight
that whereas classic TCP

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:49.550
cubic for example,
will back off when

00:12:49.550 --> 00:12:51.680
it notices loss in
the network, BBR

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:53.630
uses the insight that
in a modern network,

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:55.088
the routers have
very deep buffers.

00:12:55.088 --> 00:12:56.755
And so you can actually
tell that things

00:12:56.755 --> 00:12:59.390
are starting to get full by
noticing the packets space out

00:12:59.390 --> 00:13:00.018
more and more.

00:13:00.018 --> 00:13:02.060
And you can use that as
a signal that the network

00:13:02.060 --> 00:13:04.340
is congested and back off.

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:07.420
And it turns out in the modern
internet for many use cases,

00:13:07.420 --> 00:13:09.290
it's actually a much
more reliable signal

00:13:09.290 --> 00:13:11.060
of the available
throughput on a link.

00:13:11.060 --> 00:13:13.913
And so what we can actually see
here is that when we use BBR,

00:13:13.913 --> 00:13:15.830
we can achieve significantly
higher throughput

00:13:15.830 --> 00:13:17.663
in the presence of the
kind of sporadic loss

00:13:17.663 --> 00:13:21.380
that happens on the internet
of over the same RTT.

00:13:21.380 --> 00:13:22.880
And so those were
kind of two things

00:13:22.880 --> 00:13:24.730
that I wanted to dive deeper on.

00:13:24.730 --> 00:13:27.343
But again, I think it's
important to know at Google

00:13:27.343 --> 00:13:28.760
that the innovation
in the network

00:13:28.760 --> 00:13:30.802
is something that we have
been doing consistently

00:13:30.802 --> 00:13:33.390
over the last decade.

00:13:33.390 --> 00:13:35.660
A couple of things that are
particularly cool for CBN

00:13:35.660 --> 00:13:37.460
are our Google global
cache deployments

00:13:37.460 --> 00:13:38.502
that live all around the.

00:13:38.502 --> 00:13:39.110
World

00:13:39.110 --> 00:13:41.630
Espresso, which is our
internet facing software

00:13:41.630 --> 00:13:42.970
defined networking stack.

00:13:42.970 --> 00:13:45.480
And so if you can
imagine in real time,

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:47.840
one of the things that
we do is we figure out

00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:50.853
exactly how traffic is going
to flow across that network

00:13:50.853 --> 00:13:52.520
that Google operates,
and how it's going

00:13:52.520 --> 00:13:53.970
to flow through the internet.

00:13:53.970 --> 00:13:55.345
We try to make
sure that we never

00:13:55.345 --> 00:13:57.680
overload any link we monitor
in real time, again, all

00:13:57.680 --> 00:13:59.970
in software, every minute,
to see where there's

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:01.970
any degradations in
performance to certain parts

00:14:01.970 --> 00:14:02.690
of the internet.

00:14:02.690 --> 00:14:04.490
And how we can use
that broad footprint

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:06.290
to route traffic around it.

00:14:06.290 --> 00:14:07.880
And so this also
I think has been

00:14:07.880 --> 00:14:10.460
one of the key things, this
proprietary traffic management

00:14:10.460 --> 00:14:13.340
system, that had enabled
us to really push the bar

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:14.970
and make things
better at Google.

00:14:14.970 --> 00:14:18.200
So with that, I
wanted to hand it over

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:21.110
to see, OK, how this amazing
infrastructure works,

00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:22.850
so we'd like to think.

00:14:22.850 --> 00:14:24.740
And how do you actually
set it up and how

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:28.150
do you get all that simplicity.

00:14:28.150 --> 00:14:30.740
SAURABH GUPTA: Thank you.

00:14:30.740 --> 00:14:31.910
So set up and use.

00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:32.970
How do you get started.

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:35.200
How do you make
this work for you.

00:14:35.200 --> 00:14:37.010
So let's take a look at that.

00:14:37.010 --> 00:14:39.230
First, we'll start
by seeing what

00:14:39.230 --> 00:14:43.940
a traditional CDN looks like,
and how it thinks of the world.

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:45.850
You will start with--

00:14:45.850 --> 00:14:47.950
there is actually a
gray box around here,

00:14:47.950 --> 00:14:50.840
so use your imagination.

00:14:50.840 --> 00:14:54.260
There is a gray box, which
is regional data center.

00:14:54.260 --> 00:14:56.510
And in that, you have some
kind of virtual machine

00:14:56.510 --> 00:14:59.030
or some kind of an
object store, which

00:14:59.030 --> 00:15:01.430
is fronted by a load
balancer, which is then

00:15:01.430 --> 00:15:03.710
fronted by a regional IP.

00:15:03.710 --> 00:15:06.380
That regional IP is
advertised through

00:15:06.380 --> 00:15:08.630
the regional public network.

00:15:08.630 --> 00:15:12.200
And that's why if you have sort
of multi region deployments,

00:15:12.200 --> 00:15:14.660
you will then have
that same deployment

00:15:14.660 --> 00:15:16.280
across different data centers.

00:15:16.280 --> 00:15:18.230
Each one will have
their own load balancer.

00:15:18.230 --> 00:15:20.888
Each one will have their
own sort of regional IP.

00:15:20.888 --> 00:15:23.180
And then they will plug into
the public network that is

00:15:23.180 --> 00:15:26.340
proximal to that data center.

00:15:26.340 --> 00:15:30.260
Which means you really need
to layer a wrapper of caching

00:15:30.260 --> 00:15:31.490
around all this, right?

00:15:31.490 --> 00:15:33.440
So you will then
have a separate layer

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:36.530
of caching that
exists at the edge.

00:15:36.530 --> 00:15:38.750
Which means if a user,
let's say, in LA,

00:15:38.750 --> 00:15:42.373
wants to connect
to your content,

00:15:42.373 --> 00:15:43.790
if they're using
your domain name,

00:15:43.790 --> 00:15:45.800
they will look up some
kind of DNS entry.

00:15:45.800 --> 00:15:51.060
They will find the IP that
creates a path to your cache

00:15:51.060 --> 00:15:51.682
endpoint.

00:15:51.682 --> 00:15:53.390
And then also, the
caching infrastructure

00:15:53.390 --> 00:15:55.680
will need to know where
to get that data from.

00:15:55.680 --> 00:15:57.440
So at some level,
they will have to back

00:15:57.440 --> 00:15:59.210
into the public network.

00:15:59.210 --> 00:16:01.370
Again, go through
their public IP,

00:16:01.370 --> 00:16:03.410
find the load
balancer et cetera,

00:16:03.410 --> 00:16:05.330
and serve that content
back out the same path.

00:16:05.330 --> 00:16:07.310
So really, there's lots of hops.

00:16:07.310 --> 00:16:09.410
This is going through
the public network.

00:16:09.410 --> 00:16:11.360
This is going to
a public network.

00:16:11.360 --> 00:16:13.790
And also, at the edge,
you have a fair amount

00:16:13.790 --> 00:16:16.940
of complexity in
configuration in terms of IP

00:16:16.940 --> 00:16:18.317
based load balancing
or DNS based

00:16:18.317 --> 00:16:19.400
load balancing, et cetera.

00:16:23.210 --> 00:16:24.730
So this is kind
of like a summary.

00:16:24.730 --> 00:16:26.140
Multiple DNS lookups.

00:16:26.140 --> 00:16:31.000
CDN is a separate system from
your serving infrastructure.

00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:36.030
Regional constructs with
again, regional IPs.

00:16:36.030 --> 00:16:38.790
If you look at how
Google approached it,

00:16:38.790 --> 00:16:44.550
we said, again, because we
have that global construct

00:16:44.550 --> 00:16:53.555
to our VPCs, our load balancers
actually sit at the edge.

00:16:53.555 --> 00:16:55.680
I mean, part of their
infrastructure is at the edge

00:16:55.680 --> 00:16:57.190
itself.

00:16:57.190 --> 00:17:01.140
And this is our Google Fiber
that Brian gave you a preview

00:17:01.140 --> 00:17:01.890
on.

00:17:01.890 --> 00:17:07.359
So when you have to
get data out of Google,

00:17:07.359 --> 00:17:09.960
you have a global anycast
IP, so it's broadcast out

00:17:09.960 --> 00:17:12.869
of all these end points, and
you can figure out the fastest

00:17:12.869 --> 00:17:15.270
path to that end point.

00:17:15.270 --> 00:17:18.599
You then ingress at
the edge location.

00:17:18.599 --> 00:17:20.190
The load balancer
figures out how

00:17:20.190 --> 00:17:23.880
to route the traffic
efficiently through the backbone

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:26.819
to where the data
is actually stored.

00:17:26.819 --> 00:17:29.040
You traverse this
private fiber path.

00:17:29.040 --> 00:17:30.750
Again, also, to the private.

00:17:30.750 --> 00:17:33.060
You traverse this
private fiber path

00:17:33.060 --> 00:17:34.200
to the correct data center.

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:38.490
You get your data, and you
then traverse that back out.

00:17:38.490 --> 00:17:41.800
So what you get are distributed
constructs all the way through,

00:17:41.800 --> 00:17:42.300
right?

00:17:42.300 --> 00:17:45.600
You get distributed load
balancing, distributed caching,

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:48.280
and distributed SSL at the edge.

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:50.260
There is another cool
thing that happens here.

00:17:50.260 --> 00:17:52.305
So the SSL will
be advertised out

00:17:52.305 --> 00:17:54.180
at this end point, so
the your front end will

00:17:54.180 --> 00:17:56.238
have your domain based SSL.

00:17:56.238 --> 00:17:57.780
And let's say you
say, you know what,

00:17:57.780 --> 00:18:03.300
I want this to simply serve HTTP
to my load balancer front end.

00:18:03.300 --> 00:18:06.740
That will still be an
encrypted transit path.

00:18:06.740 --> 00:18:08.740
Google is just going to
encrypt that in transit,

00:18:08.740 --> 00:18:10.323
so you don't have
to explicitly create

00:18:10.323 --> 00:18:13.860
an SSL infrastructure to have
this edge traffic reach here.

00:18:13.860 --> 00:18:14.610
You obviously can.

00:18:14.610 --> 00:18:15.990
You can have self
signed keys, you

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:17.448
can have all those
types of things.

00:18:17.448 --> 00:18:20.340
But in general, by
putting SSL at the edge,

00:18:20.340 --> 00:18:22.830
you start to get all the
benefits of QUIC and HTTP/2,

00:18:22.830 --> 00:18:25.490
the fast handshake
negotiation, et cetera.

00:18:25.490 --> 00:18:27.240
And you get a fairly
secured [INAUDIBLE]..

00:18:27.240 --> 00:18:29.460
You get a secure pipe all
the way through to where

00:18:29.460 --> 00:18:32.570
your data is stored.

00:18:32.570 --> 00:18:35.370
So let's actually see
how some of this works.

00:18:35.370 --> 00:18:38.660
So here, I have an origin,
which is a bucket of data.

00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:39.770
Can we switch to the demo?

00:18:43.250 --> 00:18:46.020
And here's the bucket.

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:47.940
So the bucket is public.

00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:50.610
It's called public as
a data in it is public.

00:18:50.610 --> 00:18:52.460
But the bucket
itself is private.

00:18:52.460 --> 00:18:54.410
You cannot list it
or sort of browse it.

00:18:57.290 --> 00:19:00.230
And here, we have all
the different objects

00:19:00.230 --> 00:19:02.580
that we will be serving.

00:19:02.580 --> 00:19:08.420
So these are all
MPEG Dash chunks.

00:19:08.420 --> 00:19:14.160
And here you can see
sort of the metadata

00:19:14.160 --> 00:19:19.580
for it, where at
the object level,

00:19:19.580 --> 00:19:22.650
I'm setting up
the cache control.

00:19:22.650 --> 00:19:24.590
So there, I've said
this is public.

00:19:24.590 --> 00:19:28.490
And the TTL is about 20 minutes.

00:19:33.870 --> 00:19:36.380
So here you've seen the bucket.

00:19:36.380 --> 00:19:39.170
You've seen the chunks.

00:19:39.170 --> 00:19:42.980
And you saw how we set the
metadata at the object level

00:19:42.980 --> 00:19:47.480
to market public with
a certain cache value.

00:19:47.480 --> 00:19:51.010
So now, let's actually
set up this infrastructure

00:19:51.010 --> 00:19:52.670
and see what it looks like.

00:19:52.670 --> 00:19:55.000
So we'll be going back
and forth between the demo

00:19:55.000 --> 00:19:57.020
and the slides for a bit.

00:19:57.020 --> 00:20:00.590
So let's create the any
cast IP that you just saw.

00:20:00.590 --> 00:20:02.830
So if I go to my VPC networks--

00:20:02.830 --> 00:20:04.330
and again, I'll
just take one moment

00:20:04.330 --> 00:20:06.455
to highlight what this
looks like in real life when

00:20:06.455 --> 00:20:07.880
you log into the console.

00:20:07.880 --> 00:20:11.600
You will see here it's one
VPC, that's my default VPC,

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:15.700
but it's got IP addresses
in every single region.

00:20:15.700 --> 00:20:19.370
Which means if I need to send
traffic from here to there,

00:20:19.370 --> 00:20:21.760
I don't really need to do
any kind of transit link.

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:23.320
I just go from one 10.

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:24.250
address to another 10.

00:20:24.250 --> 00:20:26.260
address, and it just traverses.

00:20:26.260 --> 00:20:29.890
So that's sort of the whole
global network and premium

00:20:29.890 --> 00:20:32.390
network and all those constructs
that we were talking about.

00:20:32.390 --> 00:20:35.200
So let's go ahead and
create one of those fancy IP

00:20:35.200 --> 00:20:36.790
addresses we were talking about.

00:20:36.790 --> 00:20:39.520
Reserve static IP address.

00:20:39.520 --> 00:20:45.150
And let's call this
the Demo CDN IP.

00:20:45.150 --> 00:20:48.090
We want the premium
network, IPv4, that's fine.

00:20:48.090 --> 00:20:52.270
And we want a global ID.

00:20:52.270 --> 00:20:54.240
So let's go ahead
and click Reserve.

00:20:54.240 --> 00:20:56.640
It's going to take a few
seconds while it spins.

00:20:56.640 --> 00:20:59.693
And it's going to give us
after that an IP address

00:20:59.693 --> 00:21:00.360
that we can use.

00:21:02.940 --> 00:21:06.370
Can we switch to slides?

00:21:06.370 --> 00:21:07.310
Thanks.

00:21:07.310 --> 00:21:09.050
So we did we did that part.

00:21:09.050 --> 00:21:14.510
We figured out a way that
a end client can look up

00:21:14.510 --> 00:21:16.850
an IP address and get to us.

00:21:16.850 --> 00:21:19.050
But we didn't
create a DNS entry.

00:21:19.050 --> 00:21:20.030
So let's do that next.

00:21:23.860 --> 00:21:25.800
So here are IP
addresses created.

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:26.920
I have the address.

00:21:26.920 --> 00:21:28.930
Let's copy that.

00:21:28.930 --> 00:21:34.180
Let's go to our network
services, Cloud DNS.

00:21:34.180 --> 00:21:37.570
And I have a CDN zone
where on a certain domain,

00:21:37.570 --> 00:21:40.210
I've sort of directed
the name servers here.

00:21:40.210 --> 00:21:43.630
So I'll add a record set.

00:21:43.630 --> 00:21:47.250
And I'm going to give
it, let's say, demo.

00:21:47.250 --> 00:21:51.960
And I'm going to put in the
anycast IP that we had captured

00:21:51.960 --> 00:21:53.010
and we hit Create.

00:21:55.600 --> 00:22:01.562
So here I now have a DNS
entry that maps to that IP

00:22:01.562 --> 00:22:02.770
address that we just created.

00:22:08.690 --> 00:22:12.000
So our front end is
kind of sort of done.

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:13.680
Now let's create
the load balancer.

00:22:13.680 --> 00:22:16.560
So the load balancer is going
to do a couple of things.

00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:21.670
It's going to say,
here's my front end.

00:22:21.670 --> 00:22:25.750
I want you to serve
out of this IP address.

00:22:25.750 --> 00:22:30.317
And we need to attach the SSL
that represents that domain

00:22:30.317 --> 00:22:31.400
name that we just created.

00:22:34.062 --> 00:22:34.770
So let's do that.

00:22:38.230 --> 00:22:41.530
So here's in network services,
here's my load balancer.

00:22:41.530 --> 00:22:43.320
Click.

00:22:43.320 --> 00:22:45.340
And let's create
a load balancer.

00:22:45.340 --> 00:22:47.340
And of course, this is
going to be an HTTPS load

00:22:47.340 --> 00:22:49.180
balancer, so let's click that.

00:22:49.180 --> 00:22:51.570
Select start configuration.

00:22:51.570 --> 00:22:54.040
And we were going
to do the front end.

00:22:54.040 --> 00:23:00.890
So here, let's call it demo
CDN load balancer front end.

00:23:00.890 --> 00:23:03.170
We want HTTPS.

00:23:03.170 --> 00:23:05.540
And it's going to
be using premium.

00:23:05.540 --> 00:23:07.430
And we created this
IP address, so it's

00:23:07.430 --> 00:23:09.670
going to let us select that.

00:23:09.670 --> 00:23:10.320
443.

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:12.530
Now we need to attach
the certificate.

00:23:12.530 --> 00:23:14.250
Where's that going to come from?

00:23:14.250 --> 00:23:17.280
So let's create a new one.

00:23:17.280 --> 00:23:26.830
So let's call it demo
CDN lb SSL certificate.

00:23:26.830 --> 00:23:30.020
And here I can actually
just select Google managed.

00:23:30.020 --> 00:23:33.700
So I really don't have to figure
out what was that open SSL

00:23:33.700 --> 00:23:38.230
command, or how do
I create my CSR,

00:23:38.230 --> 00:23:41.480
and where do I send it,
and those types of things.

00:23:41.480 --> 00:23:47.250
And it makes it really easy
to get an SSL up and running.

00:23:50.410 --> 00:23:52.210
And so here's a managed SSL.

00:23:52.210 --> 00:23:54.893
I hit Create.

00:23:54.893 --> 00:23:56.810
And that starts to create
the certificate that

00:23:56.810 --> 00:24:00.830
will then get attached to
my front end load balancer.

00:24:00.830 --> 00:24:04.940
So here, I'm saying the
SSL policy's defaults

00:24:04.940 --> 00:24:07.050
and QUIC negotiation
is automatic.

00:24:07.050 --> 00:24:09.650
So without really doing
a lot, the front end

00:24:09.650 --> 00:24:11.360
will automatically
to try to detect

00:24:11.360 --> 00:24:13.370
how advanced your
client is, and if it

00:24:13.370 --> 00:24:16.460
can support some of these
more advanced protocols.

00:24:16.460 --> 00:24:18.290
And it will negotiate
the fastest way

00:24:18.290 --> 00:24:23.090
to get a handshake going
and establish a connection.

00:24:23.090 --> 00:24:24.680
So that's done.

00:24:24.680 --> 00:24:26.980
Let me switch.

00:24:26.980 --> 00:24:30.380
So so far, we created
the front end,

00:24:30.380 --> 00:24:34.080
and we attached the SSL to it.

00:24:34.080 --> 00:24:38.540
Now what we have to do
is create the back end.

00:24:38.540 --> 00:24:43.650
So once the traffic arrives on
that IP, and SSL is negotiated,

00:24:43.650 --> 00:24:47.180
et cetera, the traffic has to
go somewhere to get the data.

00:24:47.180 --> 00:24:48.710
Where does it go?

00:24:48.710 --> 00:24:50.730
We've created that bucket.

00:24:50.730 --> 00:24:52.850
And so we're going to make
that bucket a back end

00:24:52.850 --> 00:24:56.831
service that the load balancer
will direct traffic to.

00:24:56.831 --> 00:25:00.450
And the back, it could be
a bucket, it could be a VM,

00:25:00.450 --> 00:25:03.150
you don't really have to--

00:25:03.150 --> 00:25:05.082
I mean, you can
