All right.

00:30:33.360 --> 00:30:36.070
So we take a look.

00:30:36.070 --> 00:30:39.640
So let's also visualize
the network path,

00:30:39.640 --> 00:30:42.140
what that actually looks like.

00:30:42.140 --> 00:30:43.930
Because Brian
talked a little bit

00:30:43.930 --> 00:30:47.350
about the premium network,
and the standard network,

00:30:47.350 --> 00:30:48.940
and the path things take.

00:30:48.940 --> 00:30:50.560
So I will actually
want to show you

00:30:50.560 --> 00:30:53.560
what you just saw, what the
network path or something

00:30:53.560 --> 00:30:54.950
like that would be.

00:30:54.950 --> 00:30:56.710
So if we can switch
to the demo again.

00:30:59.530 --> 00:31:02.840
Here's sort of the exploration
of Google's network.

00:31:02.840 --> 00:31:06.040
So let's begin.

00:31:06.040 --> 00:31:09.300
And it's telling us about
standard versus regular.

00:31:09.300 --> 00:31:11.350
So let's take a look at
what public looks like.

00:31:11.350 --> 00:31:13.780
If our origin is
in Oregon, and we

00:31:13.780 --> 00:31:18.310
want to be somewhere
out here, and we hit go,

00:31:18.310 --> 00:31:19.870
traffic will egress from Oregon.

00:31:19.870 --> 00:31:20.830
It'll bounce around.

00:31:20.830 --> 00:31:23.080
It goes and takes a
nice lazily stroll

00:31:23.080 --> 00:31:26.237
through Canada, and finally
lands where you actually

00:31:26.237 --> 00:31:27.070
want to see content.

00:31:27.070 --> 00:31:30.640
And when you're traversing
the standard network

00:31:30.640 --> 00:31:34.120
or any other sort of generic
cloud provider network,

00:31:34.120 --> 00:31:35.712
this is sort of what you get.

00:31:35.712 --> 00:31:38.170
Which means if you're CDN is
like that, that's kind of what

00:31:38.170 --> 00:31:38.650
you're going to get, too.

00:31:38.650 --> 00:31:40.660
In fact, the reason
CDNs were built

00:31:40.660 --> 00:31:42.170
was because this was happening.

00:31:42.170 --> 00:31:44.800
That's why you needed something
very close to actually send

00:31:44.800 --> 00:31:48.130
that traffic to this person,
which then also meant

00:31:48.130 --> 00:31:50.530
that you have constructs such
as prewarming, precaching,

00:31:50.530 --> 00:31:51.860
et cetera, et cetera.

00:31:51.860 --> 00:31:53.980
But when you switch to
the premium network,

00:31:53.980 --> 00:31:57.250
a lot of those constructs
really start to get redefined.

00:31:57.250 --> 00:32:00.220
Because what happens is you
do take that direct path

00:32:00.220 --> 00:32:02.440
to the closest POP, and
then you have a very short

00:32:02.440 --> 00:32:04.630
public transit path.

00:32:04.630 --> 00:32:06.423
And milliseconds count, right?

00:32:06.423 --> 00:32:07.840
If you look at the
speed of light,

00:32:07.840 --> 00:32:11.050
each millisecond is about
186 miles of transit.

00:32:11.050 --> 00:32:13.060
So if you're shaving
milliseconds off

00:32:13.060 --> 00:32:15.610
in this transit, it
allows that single POP

00:32:15.610 --> 00:32:17.095
to serve a much wider radius.

00:32:19.730 --> 00:32:22.400
So when you do that sort of
optimal curve math and all

00:32:22.400 --> 00:32:26.120
that, you start to get some
very interesting results,

00:32:26.120 --> 00:32:30.260
and you start to really rethink
how you think of CDNs, not

00:32:30.260 --> 00:32:33.170
just in terms of how many
nodes do you have, but really,

00:32:33.170 --> 00:32:34.310
what's behind those nodes.

00:32:34.310 --> 00:32:36.268
How are they actually
establishing connections,

00:32:36.268 --> 00:32:37.857
how are they getting
their data, how

00:32:37.857 --> 00:32:39.690
are they connecting
with ISPs, and so forth.

00:32:42.245 --> 00:32:43.370
Let's switch to the slides.

00:32:47.930 --> 00:32:53.690
One interesting thing is
also talking about anycast.

00:32:53.690 --> 00:32:58.002
So I started using
GCP back in 2009.

00:32:58.002 --> 00:32:59.960
I started with App Engine,
thank you very much.

00:32:59.960 --> 00:33:03.500
I was actually a
customer at some point.

00:33:03.500 --> 00:33:06.020
And that was all I knew.

00:33:06.020 --> 00:33:08.070
Like, hey, this is how
it's supposed to work.

00:33:08.070 --> 00:33:10.650
Why would you do
it any other way.

00:33:10.650 --> 00:33:12.830
But anycast really
makes it super easy,

00:33:12.830 --> 00:33:15.290
because you get
from where you are

00:33:15.290 --> 00:33:18.020
to where you want to go
generally in a single hop

00:33:18.020 --> 00:33:20.420
or very close to a single hop.

00:33:20.420 --> 00:33:21.980
And you can compare
that to access

00:33:21.980 --> 00:33:24.320
over a regional
external IP address.

00:33:24.320 --> 00:33:25.820
And again, we saw
how simple it was.

00:33:25.820 --> 00:33:27.758
We just create a
single DNS entry,

00:33:27.758 --> 00:33:30.050
and all our traffic would
flow through that distributed

00:33:30.050 --> 00:33:31.220
infrastructure.

00:33:31.220 --> 00:33:35.150
So we'll take a look at
how anycast actually works.

00:33:35.150 --> 00:33:38.590
And this is not necessarily
CDN, but it's actually

00:33:38.590 --> 00:33:45.010
showing if you do a trace
route from let's say,

00:33:45.010 --> 00:33:49.060
a VM on a standard network
in let's say, Frankfurt--

00:33:49.060 --> 00:33:52.420
if you do the [? MTR ?]
on that load balancer IP,

00:33:52.420 --> 00:33:57.990
you will hit that endpoint
in essentially, a single hop.

00:33:57.990 --> 00:34:00.000
My content is in the states.

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:03.000
But it will still get served out
of this infrastructure, which

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:06.600
is the single hop away from
that user in Frankfurt.

00:34:06.600 --> 00:34:11.310
If you look at
non-anycast transit, which

00:34:11.310 --> 00:34:15.060
means the Frankfurt VM has to
leverage a standard network,

00:34:15.060 --> 00:34:19.022
bounce around the
internet, and go to LA,

00:34:19.022 --> 00:34:20.730
you will have a lot
of hops in the middle

00:34:20.730 --> 00:34:22.620
before you actually get there.

00:34:22.620 --> 00:34:24.750
And if you start to add up
the milliseconds and all

00:34:24.750 --> 00:34:29.449
the extra transit time, it
really makes a difference.

00:34:29.449 --> 00:34:31.239
And of course, it
was easy to set up.

00:34:31.239 --> 00:34:34.550
So we saw that already.

00:34:34.550 --> 00:34:38.360
The other thing that the CDN
does is automatic optimization.

00:34:38.360 --> 00:34:41.290
So whether it's 1
byte or 5 terabytes,

00:34:41.290 --> 00:34:44.840
we can handle objects
anywhere in that range.

00:34:44.840 --> 00:34:47.380
And we will automatically
optimize whether we

00:34:47.380 --> 00:34:49.480
should put in SSD or HDD.

00:34:49.480 --> 00:34:51.670
So you don't really have
to say this URL map,

00:34:51.670 --> 00:34:53.770
because it has videos,
please put that in HDD.

00:34:53.770 --> 00:34:57.380
This has smaller images,
please put that in SSD.

00:34:57.380 --> 00:34:59.548
We'll just sort of see
the content [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:59.548 --> 00:35:00.340
headers, et cetera.

00:35:00.340 --> 00:35:02.530
We'll figure all that
out, and it'll be done.

00:35:05.140 --> 00:35:08.460
The content chunking is
also handled automatically.

00:35:08.460 --> 00:35:12.630
So which means if we get
a byte range request,

00:35:12.630 --> 00:35:15.750
we will translate that byte
range request into the chunk

00:35:15.750 --> 00:35:17.190
requests that we need.

00:35:17.190 --> 00:35:20.100
We will go get those chunks,
cache them appropriately,

00:35:20.100 --> 00:35:22.290
see what we have already
from an internal manifest,

00:35:22.290 --> 00:35:24.810
and then serve the correct
stitched together byte range

00:35:24.810 --> 00:35:25.360
request.

00:35:25.360 --> 00:35:28.308
So if your byte range
is spanning two chunks,

00:35:28.308 --> 00:35:29.850
we will take care
of that implicitly.

00:35:29.850 --> 00:35:33.325
Like, there's really no extra
configuration or manipulation

00:35:33.325 --> 00:35:33.950
you have to do.

00:35:36.720 --> 00:35:40.450
BRIAN ROGAN: So let's talk
a little bit about security.

00:35:40.450 --> 00:35:44.470
For that, I'm going to
hand it over to Brian.

00:35:44.470 --> 00:35:47.650
So let's talk about how we
think about security at Google.

00:35:47.650 --> 00:35:50.860
First of all, we have always
believed that the internet

00:35:50.860 --> 00:35:51.900
should be SSL.

00:35:51.900 --> 00:35:53.770
And so we kind of
start everything

00:35:53.770 --> 00:35:54.965
here with no cost SSL.

00:35:54.965 --> 00:35:57.340
That shouldn't be a feature
you pay for, your site should

00:35:57.340 --> 00:35:58.120
be secure.

00:35:58.120 --> 00:36:00.220
That's the way the
internet should be.

00:36:00.220 --> 00:36:04.060
We make it easy for you to make
new certs with Google Managed

00:36:04.060 --> 00:36:05.140
Certs.

00:36:05.140 --> 00:36:10.360
And everything's
encrypted from our GFEs,

00:36:10.360 --> 00:36:12.520
from our front end, all
the way to your back end.

00:36:12.520 --> 00:36:17.900
So you know that there's an end
to end encrypted experience.

00:36:17.900 --> 00:36:21.180
We have for signed URLs, which
help you secure your content,

00:36:21.180 --> 00:36:23.130
so that you can make
sure which requests

00:36:23.130 --> 00:36:24.900
are allowed to be there.

00:36:24.900 --> 00:36:30.300
You can generate your signed
URLs offline with custom keys

00:36:30.300 --> 00:36:33.540
or with keys that we provide.

00:36:33.540 --> 00:36:36.960
And we actually do that say,
in the case of live video

00:36:36.960 --> 00:36:41.760
or in the case of chunked video,
in the manifest to every chunk

00:36:41.760 --> 00:36:42.800
request.

00:36:42.800 --> 00:36:45.090
And the signature is validated
right at the CDN edge.

00:36:45.090 --> 00:36:49.640
So if there's a request
that shouldn't get through,

00:36:49.640 --> 00:36:53.610
they'll get blocked
right where they started.

00:36:53.610 --> 00:36:56.310
You can see what that looks
like in the context of Saurabh's

00:36:56.310 --> 00:36:57.145
demo.

00:36:57.145 --> 00:36:58.770
We've actually signed
every chunk right

00:36:58.770 --> 00:37:00.630
in the manifest file.

00:37:00.630 --> 00:37:02.890
And that's pretty easy to do.

00:37:02.890 --> 00:37:05.483
We have kind of example
code linked to here.

00:37:05.483 --> 00:37:07.650
And again, that that's all
going to be checked right

00:37:07.650 --> 00:37:08.400
where it happened.

00:37:11.611 --> 00:37:15.770
Let's Talk a little bit about
DDoS attacks and DDoS defense.

00:37:15.770 --> 00:37:18.610
So we have a product
called Cloud Armor

00:37:18.610 --> 00:37:20.200
that you can use
with CDN that helps

00:37:20.200 --> 00:37:25.090
you to write more complex
rules, and to be able to block

00:37:25.090 --> 00:37:26.650
DDoS attacks where they start.

00:37:26.650 --> 00:37:29.230
But at the end of the
day, to even get that far,

00:37:29.230 --> 00:37:30.910
you need to make sure that you
have an infrastructure that's

00:37:30.910 --> 00:37:33.100
scaled up and ready to
handle the scale of attacks

00:37:33.100 --> 00:37:34.810
that we see on the
internet today.

00:37:34.810 --> 00:37:37.630
So step one of DDoS
prevention at Google

00:37:37.630 --> 00:37:39.488
is to out scale
this attack, right?

00:37:39.488 --> 00:37:41.530
And that's where making
sure that you write along

00:37:41.530 --> 00:37:43.450
with a provider who
has enormous scale

00:37:43.450 --> 00:37:46.000
is a really important
consideration.

00:37:46.000 --> 00:37:49.390
And we have a great
quote here from Damian,

00:37:49.390 --> 00:37:52.570
when the folks that looks
at DDoS at Google, basically

00:37:52.570 --> 00:37:55.030
saying that absorbing
the largest attacks

00:37:55.030 --> 00:37:57.460
actually requires
the bandwidth needed

00:37:57.460 --> 00:38:01.120
to watch half a million YouTube
videos at the same time in HD.

00:38:01.120 --> 00:38:02.968
And we don't see that
trend slowing at all.

00:38:02.968 --> 00:38:04.510
If anything, we see
the scale of DDoS

00:38:04.510 --> 00:38:06.520
attacks that Google
sort of hockey sticking,

00:38:06.520 --> 00:38:08.020
and that's the thing
that we've been

00:38:08.020 --> 00:38:13.460
planning for in our products
and in our infrastructure.

00:38:13.460 --> 00:38:15.340
And this is just an
example of what that

00:38:15.340 --> 00:38:17.390
looks like in your project.

00:38:17.390 --> 00:38:19.450
So we ran an
experiment just to see,

00:38:19.450 --> 00:38:22.060
can we sustain a
million [? RPS ?]

00:38:22.060 --> 00:38:24.420
DDoS attack on an
example project

00:38:24.420 --> 00:38:26.844
in a load balancer in GCLP.

00:38:26.844 --> 00:38:30.670
And you can kind of see that
through the entire duration

00:38:30.670 --> 00:38:32.710
of the attack,
everything kind of stays

00:38:32.710 --> 00:38:34.450
running smooth and steady.

00:38:36.552 --> 00:38:39.010
And then I think the other
thing that's worth talking about

00:38:39.010 --> 00:38:40.870
is that because we have
this global network,

00:38:40.870 --> 00:38:44.800
and because all of your cloud
IDs are going to be anycasted,

00:38:44.800 --> 00:38:47.050
that actually makes it very
hard to concentrate a DDoS

00:38:47.050 --> 00:38:50.680
attack on any particular element
of the Google infrastructure.

00:38:50.680 --> 00:38:52.180
So the DDoS attack
will actually get

00:38:52.180 --> 00:38:54.100
absorbed across our
entire appearing surface

00:38:54.100 --> 00:38:56.980
all across the world, where
it'll be handled and dealt

00:38:56.980 --> 00:38:58.138
with there.

00:38:58.138 --> 00:38:59.680
And so again, I
think what that means

00:38:59.680 --> 00:39:01.212
is that when you
come to Google, you

00:39:01.212 --> 00:39:02.670
have an infrastructure
that's built

00:39:02.670 --> 00:39:04.337
to write out some of
the biggest attacks

00:39:04.337 --> 00:39:06.555
that we know about today.

00:39:06.555 --> 00:39:08.680
I'll hand it back to Saurabh
to just talk about how

00:39:08.680 --> 00:39:11.370
to put this all together.

00:39:11.370 --> 00:39:13.590
SAURABH GUPTA: So
winning, right?

00:39:13.590 --> 00:39:15.180
Making it work for you.

00:39:15.180 --> 00:39:20.160
We give you ease of use,
all of Google distributed

00:39:20.160 --> 00:39:22.380
infrastructure and
serving capability

00:39:22.380 --> 00:39:24.030
delivered to a single checkbox.

00:39:24.030 --> 00:39:27.328
It gives you the anycast
IP, the content services,

00:39:27.328 --> 00:39:29.370
the monitoring and logging,
programmatic control.

00:39:29.370 --> 00:39:30.930
Everything is done
to an API so you

00:39:30.930 --> 00:39:32.940
can script out all
the work that we just

00:39:32.940 --> 00:39:35.610
showed through user interface.

00:39:35.610 --> 00:39:38.230
The scale of course,
is billions of users.

00:39:38.230 --> 00:39:42.360
If you look at, again, the
next most utilized services,

00:39:42.360 --> 00:39:45.520
they do not have the same
order of magnitude of scale

00:39:45.520 --> 00:39:47.650
that we routinely work at.

00:39:47.650 --> 00:39:50.110
And the critical thing here
is ramp without warm up.

00:39:50.110 --> 00:39:54.480
So if you have content, if you
have any type of end streaming

00:39:54.480 --> 00:39:58.330
system, and you do not know
what demand is going to be,

00:39:58.330 --> 00:40:01.920
it's very hard to kind of get
the right provisioning upfront.

00:40:01.920 --> 00:40:03.960
But with Cloud CDN,
you really don't have

00:40:03.960 --> 00:40:05.610
to worry about that the scale.

00:40:05.610 --> 00:40:08.490
We'll just be transparent
and automatic.

00:40:08.490 --> 00:40:09.420
Again, footprint.

00:40:09.420 --> 00:40:11.940
So it's not just how
many can be served,

00:40:11.940 --> 00:40:13.830
but where all can we serve it.

00:40:13.830 --> 00:40:16.800
And again, the most popular
sites all across the world.

00:40:16.800 --> 00:40:19.120
Some of them, like
the Google ones,

00:40:19.120 --> 00:40:20.790
they all leverage
this infrastructure,

00:40:20.790 --> 00:40:26.143
and you get to ride the
same underlying stack.

00:40:26.143 --> 00:40:27.310
And of course, the security.

00:40:27.310 --> 00:40:30.210
So it's not any good if you
can't protect your content

00:40:30.210 --> 00:40:31.210
and your infrastructure.

00:40:31.210 --> 00:40:34.200
So you do get all the
investments that we make.

00:40:34.200 --> 00:40:35.830
You just get that
out of the box.

00:40:35.830 --> 00:40:38.160
So it's the
outscaling of attacks,

00:40:38.160 --> 00:40:44.720
the anycast distribution,
and the free SSL.

